// Each actor's own garbage collector, a part of the actors package: every actor
// carries a region of its own, kept by its Header. 'ActorGc' is that
// region's reference: its values live in the memory of the one actor that made
// them and are found, moved and freed by a copying collector that runs only
// between that actor's messages, when the actor's state and its parked 'await'
// records are the only roots. An actor allocates in its region by naming itself,
// with nothing imported beyond the actors package an actor already needs:
//
//   mod scene;
//   import actors;           // folds in 'ActorGc'; 'actors.GcStats' and the knobs are qualified
//
//   struct Node { pub n i64; pub next Option[ActorGc[mut, Node]]; }
//   alias NodeRef = ActorGc[mut, Node];
//
//   actor Keeper {
//     head Option[NodeRef];
//
//     fn init(self: &new) { *self = Self(None[NodeRef]); }
//
//     pub async fn push(self, n i64) {
//       head = Some[NodeRef](new self[Node](n, head));   // in the actor's region
//     }
//   }
//
// 'new self[T](args)' is 'self.allocRegion().alloc(T(args))': the compiler
// gives the state of every actor a method 'allocRegion', answering the actor's
// 'ActorRegion' (regionOf, below), and a value that has one leads to a region
// ('new x[T](...)', compiler/c/doc/nodes/literals.md, "Construction"). Only the
// actor's own methods, the behaviours among them, hold 'self', so only they
// allocate in its region; 'init' does after '*self' is filled. A reference into
// the region is made, held, copied and stored inside the actor, in its state, in
// locals, in a 'List' or an 'Rc' it owns, in a record parked by an 'await'; it
// is never Sendable, so it never leaves the actor in a message or a reply. A
// read-only one, from 'allocImm' (PROVISIONAL), is Shareable in a behaviour's
// 'parallel each', where nothing is allocated in the region.
// A collection made in the region, 'new self[List[T]](16)', takes the region's
// own allocate, resize and release for its block, as any CarriesRegion type
// made in a region does (A COLLECTION'S BLOCK, below). 'self.allocRegion().stats()'
// says what the region holds.
//
// WHAT AN ACTOR PAYS. Every actor has a region, but its record is made lazily:
// the actor's Header holds one pointer to it, null till the actor first asks for
// its region ('allocRegion'), and the first chunk is made at the first
// allocation. An actor that never asks pays one word in its Header, and its
// message loop one test of it. The record, and the three block functions that
// collections point at, are one malloc'd object that lives till the actor's
// death (freed after its finals), so their address never changes.
//
// HOW IT COLLECTS. The region is a list of CHUNKS the actor allocates in, a
// bump pointer through the newest. The first is 4 KiB, made when the actor
// first allocates; each next is twice the last, up to 1 MiB, or as large as a
// value that does not fit needs. A value over 4 KiB is a LARGE object, a
// block of its own that never moves. Every object has a 24-byte header:
// its type's record, its size, a link and some flags.
//
// A worker calls the collector after each message the actor handles (and
// after the rest of a behaviour that began a 'parallel each', once the loop
// has finished). It collects when the bytes allocated since the last collection
// exceed the bytes that survived it, and not under 64 KiB (the FLOOR), so a
// tiny actor does not collect constantly; 'setFloor' changes the floor for
// the process, 'setStress' collects after every message. The collection is
// Cheney's: the actor's state and each parked record are handed to the
// region's 'mark' reference by reference; 'mark' copies the object it finds
// into new chunks and rewrites the slot the reference was stored in; the new
// chunks are scanned in order, each object's references marked in turn, until
// nothing is left to scan. The old chunks are then kept whole on a SPARE list,
// up to twice the bytes that survived, and the rest freed; the next chunk a region needs is
// drawn from the list before the raw blocks layer, so a steady region does not fault fresh pages
// in every cycle. A large
// object is marked in place and scanned from a list; the unmarked ones are
// freed. There is no write barrier: nothing runs while it does.
//
// FINALIZERS. An object whose type needs finalizing (it has a 'final', or a
// field that does) is on the region's FINAL LIST from the moment it is made.
// When a collection finds one unreached, it is kept for one more cycle: it
// and what it reaches are copied like the rest, and its death runs on the copy,
// in the order the objects were made, newest first, once. So a 'final' reads
// the references its object holds, whose targets are there, finalized
// already or not; they are freed by the collection after, as the collector
// package's are. When the actor dies, after its state's own death, every
// object still on the final list is finalized the same way and the region is
// freed whole.
//
// A reference behind an 'Rc' is traced by the 'Rc' ('visit', once for each
// collection: the epoch is 1, 2, 3 ... never 0), so a cycle of them ends.
//
// A COLLECTION'S BLOCK. The region is also a MemRegion: its first field, 'region',
// points at the functions (allocate, resize, release, and core's exact size) that sit in the
// actor's Header beside the rest of the region, which never moves. A type
// declaring CarriesRegion, made in the region ('new self[List[i64]](16)'), is
// handed that region and takes its block from it, so the block is the actor's
// region's: counted in the bytes allocated since the last collection and in
// the bytes that survive one, and given back by the collection's own 'final'
// (the list's, which every list has). A block is NOT bump-allocated in the
// chunks and NOT copied by a collection: it is a block of its own, as a large
// object is, and the collection's 'ptr' is the only holder of its address, a
// raw pointer a collector has no way to rewrite (the compiler gives the
// collector no way to find a block field, so nothing says which word of a
// collection holds one). What a collection moves is the collection's own words
// (a List's four), a region object like any, which keep the block's address and
// the region's address, neither of which a collection changes. Resize is
// the raw blocks layer's, so an outgrown block goes back at once, not at the next
// collection.
// Because every collection has a 'final', one nobody reaches is finalized by the
// collector as any dead object is, and that gives its block back; at the actor's
// death, the same for every one still alive.
//
// WHAT TO KNOW. An object moves in every collection that finds it, so nothing
// holds its address across a message: a raw pointer to one is the program's own
// risk, as in any moving collector. A collection that runs between two messages
// sees no borrow. The region's own memory is the actor's: a collection is
// only ever run by the worker running that actor.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE GC LAB (a lab copy of this file, never merged): collector variants chosen
// at build time with congo's '-D NAME=value'. With none given this is the
// collector described above, unchanged. Notes: C:\src\zz-gc-lab-collector-notes.md
//
//   GC_FACTOR=2|3|4|5|6|8   collect when the heap would be this multiple of the live bytes (2 = today)
//   GC_ADAPT=<percent>      adaptive trigger: steer the factor (150..1600) toward this share of time in collection
//   GC_EMPTY=1              also collect when the actor's mailbox empties (GC_EMPTYFLOOR bytes allocated, default 8192)
//   GC_RECLAIM=0|1|2        0 copy everything (today); 1 mark in place, Immix-style blocks and lines;
//                           2 as 1 plus evacuation of sparse blocks while marking (GC_EVAC_FRAG, GC_EVAC_SPARSE percent)
//   GC_CHECK=1              after every collection, walk the live graph and verify every reference
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const factorInit = #if #define("GC_FACTOR") == 3 {300usize} #elif #define("GC_FACTOR") == 4 {400usize}
  #elif #define("GC_FACTOR") == 5 {500usize} #elif #define("GC_FACTOR") == 6 {600usize}
  #elif #define("GC_FACTOR") == 8 {800usize} #else {200usize};
// (A build fact can only be read in a '#if' condition, so each number a flag may take is listed.)
const adaptOn = #if #defined("GC_ADAPT") {true} #else {false};
const adaptTarget = #if #define("GC_ADAPT") == 1 {1usize} #elif #define("GC_ADAPT") == 2 {2usize}
  #elif #define("GC_ADAPT") == 3 {3usize} #elif #define("GC_ADAPT") == 5 {5usize}
  #elif #define("GC_ADAPT") == 8 {8usize} #elif #define("GC_ADAPT") == 10 {10usize}
  #elif #define("GC_ADAPT") == 15 {15usize} #elif #define("GC_ADAPT") == 20 {20usize}
  #elif #define("GC_ADAPT") == 25 {25usize} #elif #define("GC_ADAPT") == 30 {30usize}
  #elif #define("GC_ADAPT") == 40 {40usize} #elif #define("GC_ADAPT") == 50 {50usize} #else {10usize};
const emptyOn = #if #defined("GC_EMPTY") {true} #else {false};
const emptyFloor = #if #define("GC_EMPTYFLOOR") == 1024 {1024usize} #elif #define("GC_EMPTYFLOOR") == 4096 {4096usize}
  #elif #define("GC_EMPTYFLOOR") == 16384 {16384usize} #elif #define("GC_EMPTYFLOOR") == 65536 {65536usize}
  #elif #define("GC_EMPTYFLOOR") == 262144 {262144usize} #else {8192usize};
// GC lab, added at the harness join: GC_EMPTYPCT = 25, 50 or 75: the empty-mailbox collection waits until the bytes allocated since the last
// collection pass that per cent of the trigger's own amount (and the floor), so its cost follows the live size as the trigger's does. Unset: the
// floor alone (the first version of the variant, which collects after nearly every message on a large live set)
const emptyPct = #if #define("GC_EMPTYPCT") == 25 {25usize} #elif #define("GC_EMPTYPCT") == 50 {50usize}
  #elif #define("GC_EMPTYPCT") == 75 {75usize} #else {0usize};
const reclaimMode = #if #define("GC_RECLAIM") == 1 {1usize} #elif #define("GC_RECLAIM") == 2 {2usize} #else {0usize};
const checkOn = #if #defined("GC_CHECK") {true} #else {false};
// GC_STICKY=1 (on GC_RECLAIM=1 only): minor and full collections. Mark bits stay between collections, so a marked object is old; a minor
// collection traces from the roots and the remembered set only, and sweeps only the blocks allocated into since the last collection; a full
// collection clears the marks and traces everything. GC_NURSERY (KiB allocated between collections) 256, 1024, 4096; GC_FULLEVERY: a full
// collection after this many minors, 8, 32, 128. Old objects written to are logged by 'wrote' (the workload's barrier call).
const stickyOn = #if #defined("GC_STICKY") { reclaimMode == 1usize } #else {false};
const nurseryBytes = #if #define("GC_NURSERY") == 256 {262144usize} #elif #define("GC_NURSERY") == 4096 {4194304usize} #else {1048576usize};
const fullEvery = #if #define("GC_FULLEVERY") == 8 {8usize} #elif #define("GC_FULLEVERY") == 128 {128usize} #else {32usize};
// Evacuation: candidates are chosen when the free room inside partly used blocks passes evacFrag per cent of
// those blocks' room; a block is a candidate when under evacSparse per cent of its room is live
const evacFrag = #if #define("GC_EVAC_FRAG") == 5 {5usize}
  #elif #define("GC_EVAC_FRAG") == 10 {10usize} #elif #define("GC_EVAC_FRAG") == 30 {30usize}
  #elif #define("GC_EVAC_FRAG") == 40 {40usize} #elif #define("GC_EVAC_FRAG") == 50 {50usize}
  #elif #defined("GC_EVAC_FRAG") && #define("GC_EVAC_FRAG") == 0 {0usize} #else {20usize};
const evacSparse = #if #define("GC_EVAC_SPARSE") == 25 {25usize} #elif #define("GC_EVAC_SPARSE") == 33 {33usize}
  #elif #define("GC_EVAC_SPARSE") == 66 {66usize} #elif #define("GC_EVAC_SPARSE") == 75 {75usize}
  #elif #define("GC_EVAC_SPARSE") == 90 {90usize} #else {50usize};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The knobs: set before the first actor allocates, for the process
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Bytes allocated since the last collection that a collection waits for, at the least
const defaultFloor = 65536usize;
// The first chunk of a region, and the most a chunk grows to unless a value needs more
const firstChunkBytes = 4096usize;
const maxChunkBytes = 1048576usize;
// A value over this many bytes (header included) is a large object, never moved
const largeBytes = 4096usize;

// Every worker reads these after each message while the program may write them,
// so each is an atomic held by a 'let' global: a plain 'var' one would be a
// data race. 'floorBytes' starts at 'defaultFloor', all three set in the module's
// 'init' (actors.cone)
let floorBytes: Atomic[usize];
let stressOn: Atomic[bool];

// The environment is read once, by whichever comes first of the first actor and
// a call to 'setFloor' or 'setStress'. Those two settle it without reading it,
// so a program's own choice is not overwritten by the environment. A thread
// that arrives while another is doing this waits until it is done
let envOnce: sync.Once;

fn envSettled() {}

fn readEnvOnce() {
  let val = libc.getenv("CONE_ACTORGC_STRESS");
  if val as usize != 0usize {
    stressOn.store(true);
  }
}

// Collect no sooner than after this many bytes are allocated: the floor under
// the rule "allocated since the last collection exceeds what survived it".
// For the process, before the first actor allocates
pub fn setFloor(bytes: usize) {
  envOnce.callOnce(&envSettled);
  floorBytes.store(bytes);
}

// Collect after every message, whatever was allocated: for tests, which find a
// reference a collection missed, a stale one a move left behind, at once. The
// environment variable CONE_ACTORGC_STRESS does the same for a whole run
pub fn setStress(on: bool) {
  envOnce.callOnce(&envSettled);
  stressOn.store(on);
}

fn readEnv() {
  envOnce.callOnce(&readEnvOnce);
}

// Clock ticks (clockTicks, actors.cone) as nanoseconds
fn ticksToNs(t: usize) usize {
  let f = usize.from(clockFrequency());
  (t / f) * 1000000000usize + ((t % f) * 1000000000usize) / f
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The region: a header before each value
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const flagLarge = 1u32;      // a block of its own, never moved: 'link' chains the large objects
const flagForwarded = 2u32;  // copied, in this collection: 'link' is where
const flagMarked = 4u32;     // a large object reached in this collection
const flagOld = 8u32;        // (GC_STICKY) a large object a collection has kept: not traced by a minor one
const flagLogged = 16u32;    // (GC_STICKY) an old object in the remembered set

// The header before every value. It is a collector's data, so its fields are
// public, but a program never holds one.
pub struct ActorGc is RegionRef, Traced, ActorLocal {
  pub ty: *TypeRecord;  // the value's type record: size, alignment, finalizer, trace
  pub link: usize;      // a large object: the next one; a forwarded one: its copy
  pub size: u32;        // the object's bytes, header included, a multiple of 16
  pub flags: u32;

  // 'ActorGc[mut, T](v)', made through the actor's region ('alloc' of
  // ActorRegion), which names the actor's region first
  fn alloc(size: usize, ty: *TypeRecord) *u8 {
    let sp = target;
    target = null;
    if sp == null {
      panic("ActorGc: a value is made in an actor's region by 'new self[T](...)', never by 'ActorGc[...]()' on its own");
    }
    (sp as &mut ActorRegion).allocate(size, ty)
  }

  // A reference was found, by the state, a parked record or an object being
  // scanned: 'slot' is where it is stored. The object is copied into the new
  // chunks, once, and the slot rewritten to point at the copy; a large object
  // stays where it is and is marked
  fn mark(self: &uni ActorGc, slot: *usize) {
    let sp = curActorRegion;
    if sp == null {
      return;
    }
    let r = sp as &mut ActorRegion;
    if r.verifying {
      r.verifyRef(self as *ActorGc as usize);
    } else if reclaimMode == 0usize {
      r.markObject(self as *ActorGc, slot);
    } else {
      r.markObjectMark(self as *ActorGc, slot);
    }
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The mark variants' blocks (GC_RECLAIM 1 and 2): Immix's, a block of 32 KiB
// aligned to 32 KiB, in lines of 128 bytes. The first five lines of a block
// hold its record, a line mark per line and a mark bit per 16 bytes (the side
// table: marking writes only here and never an object's line); the objects
// are in the other 251 lines. An object is at most 4096 bytes and sits
// inside one block, its bytes on the lines it covers.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const blockBytes = 32768usize;
const lineBytes = 128usize;
const linesPerBlock = 256usize;
const firstLine = 5usize;
const blockMeta = 640usize;       // firstLine * lineBytes
const lineOff = 64usize;          // the line marks, a byte a line
const bitmapOff = 320usize;       // the mark bits, a bit per 16 bytes of the block
const flagRecyclable = 1u32;      // BlockInfo.flags: in the recycle list, not yet allocated in since the sweep
const flagCandidate = 2u32;       // BlockInfo.flags: its live objects are evacuated in this collection

// A block's record, at the block's start
struct BlockInfo {
  pub raw: usize;      // what RawBlocks gave (the block is aligned inside it)
  pub next: usize;     // the next block of the recycle list or the free list
  pub live: u32;       // bytes the last collection marked in the block
  pub flags: u32;
  pub pad0: usize;
  pub pad1: usize;
}

// Is the object at 'a' (its header) marked in its block's side table?
fn markedAt(a: usize) bool {
  let blk = a & (0usize - blockBytes);
  let idx = (a - blk) >> 4usize;
  let wp = (blk + bitmapOff + (idx >> 6usize) * 8usize) as *u64;
  ((*wp) & (1u64 << u64.from(idx & 63usize))) != 0u64
}

// A hash set of nonzero addresses in malloc'd memory (the verification's)
fn setSlot(key: usize, cap: usize) usize {
  ((key >> 4usize) ^ (key >> 12usize) ^ (key >> 21usize)) & (cap - 1usize)
}

fn setHas(tab: *usize, cap: usize, key: usize) bool {
  var i = setSlot(key, cap);
  while *(tab + i) != 0usize {
    if *(tab + i) == key {
      return true;
    }
    i = (i + 1usize) & (cap - 1usize);
  }
  false
}

// Add key; true if it was not there
fn setAdd(tab: *usize, cap: usize, key: usize) bool {
  var i = setSlot(key, cap);
  while *(tab + i) != 0usize {
    if *(tab + i) == key {
      return false;
    }
    i = (i + 1usize) & (cap - 1usize);
  }
  *(tab + i) = key;
  true
}

// A power of two of at least n, for a table that holds n entries with room
fn setCap(n: usize) usize {
  var c = 16usize;
  while c < n * 2usize {
    c = c * 2usize;
  }
  c
}

fn setMake(cap: usize) *usize {
  let t = libc.calloc(cap, mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
  if t == null {
    panic("ActorGc: out of memory for a check");
  }
  t
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The region's memory
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The space a collection is in, for 'mark'; and the space the next allocation is
// for. Thread-local: workers run actors, and so collect and allocate, at once
var @threadlocal curActorRegion: *ActorRegion = null;
var @threadlocal target: *ActorRegion = null;

// A chunk of the region: this header, then objects, each a multiple of 16
// bytes. 'next' is the next newer chunk.
struct Chunk {
  pub next: *Chunk;
  pub bytes: usize;     // the chunk's size, this header included
  pub used: usize;      // the offset of its first unused byte
  pub pad: usize;
}

// What a test or a tuning run reads of an actor's region
pub struct GcStats {
  pub collections: usize;    // collections run
  pub objects: usize;        // objects the region holds now, garbage not yet collected too
  pub bytes: usize;          // their bytes, headers included
  pub chunks: usize;         // chunks the region holds
  pub large: usize;          // of the objects, the large ones
  pub copied: usize;         // objects copied, in all the collections
  pub finalized: usize;      // finals run, in all the collections and at death
  pub freed: usize;          // large objects freed, in all
  pub allocated: usize;      // bytes allocated since the last collection
  pub survived: usize;       // bytes that survived the last collection
  pub blocks: usize;         // bytes held now in the blocks of collections made in the region
  pub spare: usize;          // bytes of old chunks kept for the next allocations (the SPARE list)
  // THE GC LAB's counters, appended. 'copied' above counts the objects a collection kept (copied, or marked
  // in place, or evacuated); these say what a collection really did
  pub nsCollecting: usize;   // nanoseconds spent in collections, in all
  pub maxPauseNs: usize;     // the longest one collection took
  pub movedObjects: usize;   // objects actually moved (copied or evacuated), in all
  pub movedBytes: usize;     // their bytes (the 'bytes copied')
  pub markedObjects: usize;  // objects marked in place, in all (mark variants)
  pub liveBytes: usize;      // heap bytes the last collection kept, large objects included (not collection blocks)
  pub heapBlocks: usize;     // chunks (copying) or blocks (mark variants) held now, spare and free ones too
  pub freeBlocks: usize;     // of the blocks, those holding nothing (mark variants)
  pub fragPercent: usize;    // free room inside partly used blocks, per cent of their room, at the last sweep
  pub evacBlocks: usize;     // blocks chosen for evacuation, in all
  pub factor: usize;         // the trigger's factor now, per cent (200 is 2x); moves only when adaptive
  pub evacCollections: usize; // collections that evacuated at least one block (GC lab, added by the lab harness)
  // GC_STICKY counters (zero in every other build)
  pub minorCollections: usize;
  pub fullCollections: usize;
  pub maxMinorPauseNs: usize;
  pub maxFullPauseNs: usize;
  pub nsMinor: usize;
  pub nsFull: usize;
  pub barrierCalls: usize;    // calls of 'wrote'
  pub remPeak: usize;         // the remembered set's largest size (objects), at a collection
  pub remLogged: usize;       // objects logged in all (a barrier call that found an old, unlogged object)
}

// An actor's region: its chunks, its large objects, its final list, and what
// the collector counts. It is a record of its own, malloc'd when the actor first
// asks for its region ('allocRegion') and freed at its death, after the finals;
// the actor's Header holds one pointer to it, null till then, so it never moves.
// It is a region value (its first field is a public '*MemRegion', the region the
// collections made in it draw their blocks from: its own 'vt', the allocate,
// resize and release below, which sit beside it IN THIS RECORD, so the address
// the collections hold is the record's and lasts as long as the actor), and
// 'new self[T](...)', which is 'self.allocRegion().alloc(T(...))', is the
// allocation below.
pub struct ActorRegion {
  pub region: *MemRegion;         // what collections made here get their blocks from: 'vt', below
  vt: MemRegion;                  // the region's three functions: SECOND, at the offset of one pointer (blockRegion)
  first: *Chunk = null;           // the oldest chunk
  last: *Chunk = null;            // the newest: allocation bumps here
  scanChunk: *Chunk = null;       // a collection's scan: the chunk it is in
  scanAt: usize = 0usize;         // and the offset in it
  large: usize = 0usize;          // the newest large object's header, or 0
  finals: *usize = null;          // the headers of the objects with a final, oldest first
  nfinals: usize = 0usize;
  capfinals: usize = 0usize;
  gray: *usize = null;            // large objects reached in this collection, not yet scanned
  ngray: usize = 0usize;
  capgray: usize = 0usize;
  allocated: usize = 0usize;      // bytes allocated since the last collection
  live: usize = 0usize;           // bytes that survived the last one
  liveNow: usize = 0usize;        // bytes a collection in progress has kept
  nobjects: usize = 0usize;
  nlarge: usize = 0usize;
  nchunks: usize = 0usize;
  bytes: usize = 0usize;
  spare: *Chunk = null;           // old chunks kept for reuse, linked by 'next' (the SPARE list)
  nspare: usize = 0usize;         // their bytes
  ncollections: usize = 0usize;
  ncopied: usize = 0usize;
  nfinalized: usize = 0usize;
  nfreed: usize = 0usize;
  blocks: usize = 0usize;         // bytes held in the blocks of collections made here
  epoch: u32 = 0u32;
  // ---- THE GC LAB ----
  factor100: usize = 0usize;      // the trigger's factor, per cent; 0 until first used (factorInit)
  lastEnd: usize = 0usize;        // clock ticks when the last collection ended (0: none yet)
  ticksTotal: usize = 0usize;     // clock ticks spent in collections
  ticksMax: usize = 0usize;       // the longest collection, in ticks
  nmoved: usize = 0usize;
  bytesMoved: usize = 0usize;
  nmarked: usize = 0usize;
  liveHeap: usize = 0usize;       // liveNow of the last collection
  fragPct: usize = 0usize;
  nevacBlocks: usize = 0usize;
  nevacColl: usize = 0usize;
  // GC_STICKY
  rem: *usize = null;             // the remembered set: headers of old objects written to since the last collection
  nrem: usize = 0usize;
  caprem: usize = 0usize;
  remPeak: usize = 0usize;
  nlogged: usize = 0usize;
  nbarrier: usize = 0usize;
  tch: *usize = null;             // the blocks the allocator took since the last collection (the minor sweep's)
  ntch: usize = 0usize;
  captch: usize = 0usize;
  finalsOld: usize = 0usize;      // the final list's entries before this index were there at the last collection
  stickyReady: bool = false;      // a collection has run, so the marks mean old
  sinceFull: usize = 0usize;      // minors since the last full collection
  isMinor: bool = false;
  nobjBase: usize = 0usize;
  nminor: usize = 0usize;
  nfull: usize = 0usize;
  ticksMinor: usize = 0usize;
  ticksFull: usize = 0usize;
  ticksMinorMax: usize = 0usize;
  ticksFullMax: usize = 0usize;
  verifying: bool = false;        // GC_CHECK: a verification walk is running, 'mark' checks instead of marking
  // mark variants: the blocks (GC_RECLAIM 1 and 2)
  bk: *usize = null;              // every block held: its address
  nbk: usize = 0usize;
  capbk: usize = 0usize;
  recycle: usize = 0usize;        // blocks with free lines, linked by BlockInfo.next, first to allocate in first
  freeList: usize = 0usize;       // blocks holding nothing
  nfree: usize = 0usize;
  curBlock: usize = 0usize;       // the block the allocator is in
  curLine: usize = 0usize;        // the line to look for the next hole from
  cursor: usize = 0usize;         // bump through the current hole
  limit: usize = 0usize;
  evacBlock: usize = 0usize;      // the block evacuation copies into
  evacCursor: usize = 0usize;
  evacLimit: usize = 0usize;
  evacOn: bool = false;           // this collection evacuates the candidate blocks
  // verification's scratch: a hash set of addresses
  vset: *usize = null;
  vcap: usize = 0usize;
  vcount: usize = 0usize;
  vknown: *usize = null;          // the known object starts / blocks, as a set
  vkcap: usize = 0usize;
  vkcount: usize = 0usize;
  vlarge: *usize = null;          // the large objects, as a set
  vlcap: usize = 0usize;
  vlcount: usize = 0usize;

  // A region holding nothing, made where it will stay: its three functions set
  // and its 'region' pointed at them
  fn create() *ActorRegion {
    let g = libc.malloc(mem.sizeof[ActorRegion]()) as *ActorRegion;
    if g == null {
      panic("ActorGc: out of memory for an actor's region");
    }
    mem.writeRaw(g, ActorRegion(null, MemRegion(&blockAlloc, &blockResize, &blockRelease, &blockSize)));
    (*g).region = (&mut (*g).vt) as *MemRegion;
    g
  }

  // A value made in the actor's region: 'new self[T](v)'. The next ActorGc
  // value made on this thread is in this region
  pub fn alloc[T](self: &mut, v: T) ActorGc[mut, T] {
    target = self as *ActorRegion;
    ActorGc[mut, T](v)
  }

  // A value made in the actor's region that nothing changes: the reference is
  // read-only ('imm'), so a 'parallel each' written in a behaviour may read it
  // in its passes (it is Shareable, as a 'mut' one is not). 'new self[T]' lends
  // 'mut', all a region value lends; this is the way to a read-only one.
  // PROVISIONAL: the spelling (the design chat is to say whether a permission
  // goes in the brackets, 'new self[imm, T]', or this stays)
  pub fn allocImm[T](self: &mut, v: T) ActorGc[imm, T] {
    target = self as *ActorRegion;
    ActorGc[imm, T](v)
  }

  // What the region holds and has done, for tests and tuning
  pub fn stats(self: &) GcStats {
    var f = self.factor100;
    if f == 0usize {
      f = factorInit;
    }
    GcStats(self.ncollections, self.nobjects, self.bytes, self.nchunks, self.nlarge, self.ncopied,
      self.nfinalized, self.nfreed, self.allocated, self.live, self.blocks, self.nspare,
      ticksToNs(self.ticksTotal), ticksToNs(self.ticksMax), self.nmoved, self.bytesMoved, self.nmarked,
      self.liveHeap, self.nchunks, self.nfree, self.fragPct, self.nevacBlocks, f, self.nevacColl,
      self.nminor, self.nfull, ticksToNs(self.ticksMinorMax), ticksToNs(self.ticksFullMax), ticksToNs(self.ticksMinor),
      ticksToNs(self.ticksFull), self.nbarrier, self.remPeak, self.nlogged)
  }

  // ---- A collection's block: the region's allocate, resize and release ----

  // A block of 'bytes' bytes for a collection made in the region. Not in the
  // chunks, and never moved (A COLLECTION'S BLOCK, above): the raw blocks layer's, counted
  // against the bytes allocated since the last collection
  fn blockNew(self: &mut, bytes: usize, align: usize) *u8 {
    if align > 16usize {
      panic("ActorGc: a collection's block aligned past 16 bytes is not supported");
    }
    var n = bytes;
    if n == 0usize {
      n = 1usize;
    }
    let p = RawBlocks.alloc(n, align);
    if p == null {
      return null;
    }
    self.blocks = self.blocks + bytes;
    self.allocated = self.allocated + bytes;
    p
  }

  // The block, 'old' bytes long, made 'new': in place or not. A
  // growth is counted as allocated, as much as it grew
  fn blockGrow(self: &mut, block: *u8, oldBytes: usize, newBytes: usize, align: usize) *u8 {
    if block == null {
      return self.blockNew(newBytes, align);
    }
    var n = newBytes;
    if n == 0usize {
      n = 1usize;
    }
    let p = RawBlocks.resize(block, oldBytes, n, align);
    if p == null {
      return null;
    }
    if newBytes > oldBytes {
      self.blocks = self.blocks + (newBytes - oldBytes);
      self.allocated = self.allocated + (newBytes - oldBytes);
    } else {
      self.blocks = self.blocks - (oldBytes - newBytes);
    }
    p
  }

  // The block given back: by the collection's own 'final'
  fn blockGive(self: &mut, block: *u8, bytes: usize) {
    if block == null {
      return;
    }
    RawBlocks.release(block);
    if self.blocks >= bytes {
      self.blocks = self.blocks - bytes;
    } else {
      self.blocks = 0usize;
    }
  }

  // n rounded up to a multiple of a, a power of two
  fn roundUp(n: usize, a: usize) usize {
    (n + a - 1usize) & (0usize - a)
  }

  // Where the value of an object with this type record sits after its header:
  // the {region, permission, value} layout the compiler lays out
  fn valueOffset(rec: &TypeRecord) usize {
    ActorRegion.roundUp(mem.sizeof[ActorGc](), rec.align)
  }

  // Room for an object of 'bytes' bytes (a multiple of 16): the newest chunk's
  // next bytes, or a chunk made for it
  fn bump(self: &mut, bytes: usize) *u8 {
    var c = self.last;
    if c == null || (c as &Chunk).bytes - (c as &Chunk).used < bytes {
      c = self.newChunk(bytes);
      if c == null {
        return null;
      }
    }
    let chunk = c as &mut Chunk;
    let at = (c as *u8) + chunk.used;
    chunk.used = chunk.used + bytes;
    at
  }

  // A new newest chunk with room for 'bytes': the first is small, each next
  // twice the last, to a most, unless the object needs more
  fn newChunk(self: &mut, bytes: usize) *Chunk {
    var size = firstChunkBytes;
    if self.last != null {
      size = (self.last as &Chunk).bytes * 2usize;
      if size > maxChunkBytes {
        size = maxChunkBytes;
      }
    }
    let need = bytes + mem.sizeof[Chunk]();
    if size < need {
      size = ActorRegion.roundUp(need, 4096usize);
    }
    var c = self.takeSpare(size);
    if c == null {
      c = RawBlocks.alloc(size, 16usize) as *Chunk;
      if c == null {
        return null;
      }
    } else {
      size = (c as &Chunk).bytes;
    }
    // A fresh chunk and a reused one are set up alike: nothing in it counts as used
    mem.writeRaw(c, Chunk(null, size, mem.sizeof[Chunk](), 0usize));
    if self.last == null {
      self.first = c;
    } else {
      (self.last as &mut Chunk).next = c;
    }
    self.last = c;
    self.nchunks = self.nchunks + 1usize;
    self.bytes = self.bytes + size;
    c
  }

  // The spare chunk that fits 'want' bytes best (the smallest at least that
  // large), off the list, or null
  fn takeSpare(self: &mut, want: usize) *Chunk {
    var best: *Chunk = null;
    var bestPrev: *Chunk = null;
    var prev: *Chunk = null;
    var c = self.spare;
    while c != null {
      let b = (c as &Chunk).bytes;
      if b >= want && (best == null || b < (best as &Chunk).bytes) {
        best = c;
        bestPrev = prev;
      }
      prev = c;
      c = (c as &Chunk).next;
    }
    if best == null {
      return null;
    }
    let next = (best as &Chunk).next;
    if bestPrev == null {
      self.spare = next;
    } else {
      (bestPrev as &mut Chunk).next = next;
    }
    self.nspare = self.nspare - (best as &Chunk).bytes;
    best
  }

  // The old chunks of a collection, 'oldChunks' and the next ones linked after
  // it, join the spare list; then the list is cut to 'keep' bytes (the rest is
  // given back to the raw blocks layer), so a region that shrank gives memory back
  fn spareOld(self: &mut, oldChunks: *Chunk, keep: usize) {
    var c = oldChunks;
    while c != null {
      let next = (c as &Chunk).next;
      (c as &mut Chunk).next = self.spare;
      self.spare = c;
      self.nspare = self.nspare + (c as &Chunk).bytes;
      c = next;
    }
    var kept = 0usize;
    var tail: *Chunk = null;
    c = self.spare;
    while c != null {
      let next = (c as &Chunk).next;
      let b = (c as &Chunk).bytes;
      if kept + b <= keep {
        kept = kept + b;
        tail = c;
      } else {
        if tail == null {
          self.spare = next;
        } else {
          (tail as &mut Chunk).next = next;
        }
        RawBlocks.release(c as *u8);
      }
      c = next;
    }
    self.nspare = kept;
  }

  // A new object: 'size' is the compiler's, the header and the value. Null if
  // memory is out, or the value wants an alignment past 16
  fn allocate(self: &mut, size: usize, ty: *TypeRecord) *u8 {
    let rec = ty as &TypeRecord;
    if rec.align > 16usize {
      return null;
    }
    let bytes = ActorRegion.roundUp(size, 16usize);
    if bytes > 4294967295usize {
      return null;
    }
    var p: *u8 = null;
    var flags = 0u32;
    var link = 0usize;
    if bytes > largeBytes {
      p = RawBlocks.alloc(bytes, 16usize);
      flags = flagLarge;
      link = self.large;
    } else if reclaimMode == 0usize {
      p = self.bump(bytes);
    } else {
      p = self.bumpBlock(bytes);
    }
    if p == null {
      return null;
    }
    mem.writeRaw(p as *ActorGc, ActorGc(ty, link, u32.from(bytes), flags));
    if flags == flagLarge {
      self.large = p as usize;
      self.nlarge = self.nlarge + 1usize;
    }
    if rec.flags & 1u32 != 0u32 {
      self.pushFinal(p as usize);
    }
    self.allocated = self.allocated + bytes;
    self.nobjects = self.nobjects + 1usize;
    p
  }

  // The object with a final at 'header', put last on the final list
  fn pushFinal(self: &mut, header: usize) {
    if self.nfinals == self.capfinals {
      var cap = 16usize;
      if self.capfinals != 0usize {
        cap = self.capfinals * 2usize;
      }
      let grown = libc.realloc(self.finals as *u8, cap * mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
      if grown == null {
        panic("ActorGc: out of memory for the final list");
      }
      self.finals = grown;
      self.capfinals = cap;
    }
    *(self.finals + self.nfinals) = header;
    self.nfinals = self.nfinals + 1usize;
  }

  // A large object reached, not yet scanned
  fn pushGray(self: &mut, header: usize) {
    if self.ngray == self.capgray {
      var cap = 16usize;
      if self.capgray != 0usize {
        cap = self.capgray * 2usize;
      }
      let grown = libc.realloc(self.gray as *u8, cap * mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
      if grown == null {
        panic("ActorGc: out of memory for a collection");
      }
      self.gray = grown;
      self.capgray = cap;
    }
    *(self.gray + self.ngray) = header;
    self.ngray = self.ngray + 1usize;
  }

  // ---- The collection ----

  // A reference to the object at 'header' was found at 'slot'
  fn markObject(self: &mut, header: *ActorGc, slot: *usize) {
    let h = header as &mut ActorGc;
    if h.flags & flagLarge != 0u32 {
      if h.flags & flagMarked == 0u32 {
        h.flags = h.flags | flagMarked;
        self.liveNow = self.liveNow + usize.from(h.size);
        self.pushGray(header as usize);
      }
      return;
    }
    if h.flags & flagForwarded == 0u32 {
      self.copyObject(h);
    }
    *slot = h.link + ActorRegion.valueOffset(h.ty as &TypeRecord);
  }

  // Copy the object into the new chunks, and leave its forwarding address in
  // the old header
  fn copyObject(self: &mut, h: &mut ActorGc) {
    let bytes = usize.from(h.size);
    let dst = self.bump(bytes);
    if dst == null {
      panic("ActorGc: out of memory for a collection");
    }
    libc.memcpy(dst, h as *ActorGc as *u8, bytes);
    h.flags = h.flags | flagForwarded;
    h.link = dst as usize;
    self.liveNow = self.liveNow + bytes;
    self.ncopied = self.ncopied + 1usize;
    self.nmoved = self.nmoved + 1usize;
    self.bytesMoved = self.bytesMoved + bytes;
    self.nobjects = self.nobjects + 1usize;
  }

  // Hand the references the object at 'header' holds to the region's 'mark'
  fn scanObject(self: &mut, header: usize) {
    let h = header as &ActorGc;
    let rec = h.ty as &TypeRecord;
    if rec.flags & 2u32 != 0u32 {
      let trace = rec.trace;
      trace((header + ActorRegion.valueOffset(rec)) as *u8, self.epoch);
    }
  }

  // Scan the new chunks in order, and the large objects reached, until nothing
  // is left to scan (Cheney's scan: copying appends to the chunks being scanned)
  fn drain(self: &mut) {
    var going = true;
    while going {
      going = false;
      if self.scanChunk == null && self.first != null {
        self.scanChunk = self.first;
        self.scanAt = mem.sizeof[Chunk]();
      }
      if self.scanChunk != null {
        let c = self.scanChunk as &Chunk;
        if self.scanAt < c.used {
          let at = (self.scanChunk as usize) + self.scanAt;
          self.scanAt = self.scanAt + usize.from((at as &ActorGc).size);
          self.scanObject(at);
          going = true;
        } else if c.next != null {
          self.scanChunk = c.next;
          self.scanAt = mem.sizeof[Chunk]();
          going = true;
        }
      }
      if !going && self.ngray > 0usize {
        self.ngray = self.ngray - 1usize;
        self.scanObject(*(self.gray + self.ngray));
        going = true;
      }
    }
  }

  // Has a collection found the object at 'header'?
  fn reached(header: usize) bool {
    ((header as &ActorGc).flags & (flagForwarded | flagMarked)) != 0u32
  }

  // The object's header now: where it was copied, or where it stayed
  fn moved(header: usize) usize {
    if (header as &ActorGc).flags & flagLarge != 0u32 {
      return header;
    }
    (header as &ActorGc).link
  }

  // The objects with a final that nothing reached: each is kept one more
  // cycle, copied with all it reaches, and its death run on the copy, newest
  // first, once. Those that were reached stay on the list, at their new
  // addresses.
  fn finalizeDead(self: &mut) {
    if self.nfinals == 0usize {
      return;
    }
    // Which are dead is judged before any is kept: keeping one reaches the
    // others. The list keeps those reached, in order, at their new addresses
    let dead = libc.malloc(self.nfinals * mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
    if dead == null {
      panic("ActorGc: out of memory for a collection");
    }
    var ndead = 0usize;
    var kept = 0usize;
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < self.nfinals {
      let header = *(self.finals + i);
      if ActorRegion.reached(header) {
        *(self.finals + kept) = ActorRegion.moved(header);
        kept = kept + 1usize;
      } else {
        *(dead + ndead) = header;
        ndead = ndead + 1usize;
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    self.nfinals = kept;
    // Keep the dead, with what they reach
    i = 0usize;
    while i < ndead {
      let header = *(dead + i);
      let h = header as &mut ActorGc;
      if h.flags & flagLarge != 0u32 {
        h.flags = h.flags | flagMarked;
        self.liveNow = self.liveNow + usize.from(h.size);
        self.pushGray(header);
      } else {
        self.copyObject(h);
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    self.drain();
    // Run their deaths on the copies, newest first, and take them off the list
    i = ndead;
    while i > 0usize {
      i = i - 1usize;
      let header = ActorRegion.moved(*(dead + i));
      let rec = (header as &ActorGc).ty as &TypeRecord;
      let fin = rec.finalize;
      fin((header + ActorRegion.valueOffset(rec)) as *u8);
      self.nfinalized = self.nfinalized + 1usize;
    }
    libc.free(dead as *u8);
  }

  // A collection: the state at 'st', whose type record is 'rec', and what the
  // pending table parks with it, are the roots. The variant is reclaimMode's;
  // the time it takes, the longest, and the adaptive factor are kept here
  fn collect(self: &mut, st: *u8, rec: *TypeRecord) {
    if self.factor100 == 0usize {
      self.factor100 = factorInit;
    }
    let t0 = usize.from(clockTicks());
    var minor = false;
    if stickyOn {
      if self.stickyReady && self.sinceFull < fullEvery {
        minor = true;
        self.sinceFull = self.sinceFull + 1usize;
      } else {
        self.sinceFull = 0usize;
      }
      self.isMinor = minor;
    }
    if reclaimMode == 0usize {
      self.collectCopy(st, rec);
    } else {
      self.collectMark(st, rec);
    }
    if checkOn {
      self.verify(st, rec);
    }
    let t1 = usize.from(clockTicks());
    let took = t1 - t0;
    self.ticksTotal = self.ticksTotal + took;
    if took > self.ticksMax {
      self.ticksMax = took;
    }
    if stickyOn {
      if minor {
        self.nminor = self.nminor + 1usize;
        self.ticksMinor = self.ticksMinor + took;
        if took > self.ticksMinorMax {
          self.ticksMinorMax = took;
        }
      } else {
        self.nfull = self.nfull + 1usize;
        self.ticksFull = self.ticksFull + took;
        if took > self.ticksFullMax {
          self.ticksFullMax = took;
        }
      }
    }
    if adaptOn {
      // The share of time spent collecting since the last collection ended; the factor moves
      // 25% toward fewer collections when over the target, toward more when well under it
      if self.lastEnd != 0usize {
        let since = t0 - self.lastEnd;
        let share = (took * 1000usize) / (took + since + 1usize);
        let target = adaptTarget * 10usize;
        if share > target + target / 10usize {
          self.factor100 = (self.factor100 * 5usize) / 4usize;
          if self.factor100 > 1600usize {
            self.factor100 = 1600usize;
          }
        } else if share + share / 10usize < target {
          self.factor100 = (self.factor100 * 4usize) / 5usize;
          if self.factor100 < 150usize {
            self.factor100 = 150usize;
          }
        }
      }
    }
    self.lastEnd = t1;
  }

  // The copying collection (today's): everything live is copied
  fn collectCopy(self: &mut, st: *u8, rec: *TypeRecord) {
    self.epoch = self.epoch + 1u32;
    if self.epoch == 0u32 {
      self.epoch = 1u32;
    }
    curActorRegion = self as *ActorRegion;
    let oldChunks = self.first;
    self.first = null;
    self.last = null;
    self.scanChunk = null;
    self.scanAt = 0usize;
    self.nchunks = 0usize;
    self.bytes = 0usize;
    self.liveNow = 0usize;
    // Objects are counted as they are kept: each copy here, and below the large ones
    self.nobjects = 0usize;
    // The state: its type's trace hands every reference it holds, among them
    // the records parked by 'await', to 'mark'
    let r = rec as &TypeRecord;
    if r.flags & 2u32 != 0u32 {
      let trace = r.trace;
      trace(st, self.epoch);
    }
    self.drain();
    self.finalizeDead();
    self.sweepLarge();
    // The old chunks, whole, nothing points into them now: kept for the next
    // allocations up to twice what survived (the copy to come and the allocation
    // before the next collection), the rest given back
    self.spareOld(oldChunks, self.liveNow * 2usize);
    self.ncollections = self.ncollections + 1usize;
    self.live = self.liveNow + self.blocks;
    self.liveHeap = self.liveNow;
    self.allocated = 0usize;
    self.nobjects = self.nobjects + self.nlarge;
    curActorRegion = null;
  }

  // The large objects: freed unless reached; those kept are unmarked again
  fn sweepLarge(self: &mut) {
    var prev = 0usize;
    var at = self.large;
    var nlive = 0usize;
    while at != 0usize {
      let h = at as &mut ActorGc;
      let next = h.link;
      if h.flags & flagMarked == 0u32 {
        if prev == 0usize {
          self.large = next;
        } else {
          (prev as &mut ActorGc).link = next;
        }
        RawBlocks.release(at as *u8);
        self.nfreed = self.nfreed + 1usize;
      } else {
        h.flags = h.flags & ~flagMarked;
        if stickyOn {
          h.flags = h.flags | flagOld;
        }
        nlive = nlive + 1usize;
        prev = at;
      }
      at = next;
    }
    self.nlarge = nlive;
  }

  // ---- The mark variants: allocation in blocks ----

  // A new block of 32 KiB aligned to 32 KiB, its record and side table cleared,
  // listed with the others. RawBlocks aligns to 16, so twice the room is asked
  fn newBlock(self: &mut) usize {
    let raw = RawBlocks.alloc(blockBytes * 2usize, 16usize);
    if raw == null {
      panic("ActorGc: out of memory for a block");
    }
    let base = ActorRegion.roundUp(raw as usize, blockBytes);
    libc.memset(base as *u8, 0, blockMeta);
    (base as &mut BlockInfo).raw = raw as usize;
    if self.nbk == self.capbk {
      var cap = 16usize;
      if self.capbk != 0usize {
        cap = self.capbk * 2usize;
      }
      let grown = libc.realloc(self.bk as *u8, cap * mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
      if grown == null {
        panic("ActorGc: out of memory for the block list");
      }
      self.bk = grown;
      self.capbk = cap;
    }
    *(self.bk + self.nbk) = base;
    self.nbk = self.nbk + 1usize;
    self.nchunks = self.nbk;
    self.bytes = self.nbk * blockBytes;
    base
  }

  // The block to allocate in next: a recyclable one (free lines among live
  // ones), else an empty one, else a new one
  fn takeBlock(self: &mut) usize {
    if self.recycle != 0usize {
      let b = self.recycle;
      let info = b as &mut BlockInfo;
      self.recycle = info.next;
      info.next = 0usize;
      info.flags = info.flags & ~flagRecyclable;
      self.touch(b);
      return b;
    }
    let f = self.takeFree();
    self.touch(f);
    f
  }

  // (GC_STICKY) the allocator takes block 'b': the next minor collection sweeps it
  fn touch(self: &mut, b: usize) {
    if !stickyOn {
      return;
    }
    if self.ntch == self.captch {
      var cap = 64usize;
      if self.captch != 0usize {
        cap = self.captch * 2usize;
      }
      let grown = libc.realloc(self.tch as *u8, cap * mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
      if grown == null {
        panic("ActorGc: out of memory for the touched blocks");
      }
      self.tch = grown;
      self.captch = cap;
    }
    *(self.tch + self.ntch) = b;
    self.ntch = self.ntch + 1usize;
  }

  // An empty block, from the free list or new
  fn takeFree(self: &mut) usize {
    if self.freeList != 0usize {
      let b = self.freeList;
      let info = b as &mut BlockInfo;
      self.freeList = info.next;
      info.next = 0usize;
      self.nfree = self.nfree - 1usize;
      self.nspare = self.nfree * blockBytes;
      return b;
    }
    self.newBlock()
  }

  // The next hole in block 'blk' at or after line 'from' that holds 'need'
  // bytes: the allocator's cursor and limit are set to it
  fn nextHole(self: &mut, blk: usize, from: usize, need: usize) bool {
    let marks = (blk + lineOff) as *u8;
    let lines = (need + lineBytes - 1usize) >> 7usize;
    var l = from;
    while l < linesPerBlock {
      if *(marks + l) == 0u8 {
        var e = l;
        while e < linesPerBlock && *(marks + e) == 0u8 {
          e = e + 1usize;
        }
        if e - l >= lines {
          self.cursor = blk + l * lineBytes;
          self.limit = blk + e * lineBytes;
          self.curLine = e;
          return true;
        }
        l = e;
      } else {
        l = l + 1usize;
      }
    }
    false
  }

  // Room for an object of 'bytes' bytes: bump through the current hole, else
  // the next hole that fits, in this block and then in the next ones
  fn bumpBlock(self: &mut, bytes: usize) *u8 {
    if self.cursor + bytes <= self.limit {
      let p = self.cursor;
      self.cursor = p + bytes;
      return p as *u8;
    }
    while true {
      if self.curBlock != 0usize && self.nextHole(self.curBlock, self.curLine, bytes) {
        let p = self.cursor;
        self.cursor = p + bytes;
        return p as *u8;
      }
      self.curBlock = self.takeBlock();
      self.curLine = firstLine;
      self.cursor = 0usize;
      self.limit = 0usize;
    }
    null
  }

  // Room for an evacuated object: bump through the evacuation block, an empty
  // one taken when it is full
  fn evacBump(self: &mut, bytes: usize) *u8 {
    if self.evacCursor + bytes > self.evacLimit {
      let b = self.takeFree();
      self.evacBlock = b;
      self.evacCursor = b + blockMeta;
      self.evacLimit = b + blockBytes;
    }
    let p = self.evacCursor;
    self.evacCursor = p + bytes;
    p as *u8
  }

  // ---- The mark variants: the collection ----

  // Mark the object at 'a' (its header, in the block 'blk'), 'size' bytes, if
  // not marked: its mark bit, the lines it covers, the block's live bytes; it
  // is to be scanned. True if it was not marked before
  fn markIn(self: &mut, a: usize, blk: usize, size: usize) bool {
    let idx = (a - blk) >> 4usize;
    let wp = (blk + bitmapOff + (idx >> 6usize) * 8usize) as *u64;
    let bit = 1u64 << u64.from(idx & 63usize);
    let w = *wp;
    if (w & bit) != 0u64 {
      return false;
    }
    *wp = w | bit;
    let off = a - blk;
    var l = off >> 7usize;
    let last = (off + size - 1usize) >> 7usize;
    let marks = (blk + lineOff) as *u8;
    while l <= last {
      *(marks + l) = 1u8;
      l = l + 1usize;
    }
    let info = blk as &mut BlockInfo;
    info.live = info.live + u32.from(size);
    self.liveNow = self.liveNow + size;
    self.ncopied = self.ncopied + 1usize;
    self.nobjects = self.nobjects + 1usize;
    self.pushGray(a);
    true
  }

  // A reference to the object at 'header' was found at 'slot': marked in
  // place, or (if its block is being evacuated) copied and the slot rewritten
  fn markObjectMark(self: &mut, header: *ActorGc, slot: *usize) {
    let h = header as &mut ActorGc;
    if h.flags & flagLarge != 0u32 {
      if h.flags & (flagMarked | flagOld) == 0u32 {
        h.flags = h.flags | flagMarked;
        self.liveNow = self.liveNow + usize.from(h.size);
        self.pushGray(header as usize);
      }
      return;
    }
    let a = header as usize;
    let blk = a & (0usize - blockBytes);
    if reclaimMode == 2usize {
      let info = blk as &mut BlockInfo;
      if info.flags & flagCandidate != 0u32 && !markedAt(a) {
        if h.flags & flagForwarded == 0u32 {
          self.evacuate(h);
        }
        *slot = h.link + ActorRegion.valueOffset(h.ty as &TypeRecord);
        return;
      }
    }
    if self.markIn(a, blk, usize.from(h.size)) {
      self.nmarked = self.nmarked + 1usize;
    }
  }

  // Copy the object out of a block being evacuated: forwarding is left in the
  // old header as the copying collection leaves it; the copy is marked in its
  // own block, and scanned
  fn evacuate(self: &mut, h: &mut ActorGc) {
    let bytes = usize.from(h.size);
    let dst = self.evacBump(bytes);
    libc.memcpy(dst, h as *ActorGc as *u8, bytes);
    h.flags = h.flags | flagForwarded;
    h.link = dst as usize;
    let d = dst as usize;
    self.markIn(d, d & (0usize - blockBytes), bytes);
    self.nmoved = self.nmoved + 1usize;
    self.bytesMoved = self.bytesMoved + bytes;
  }

  // Scan what marking pushed until nothing is left (large objects too)
  fn drainMark(self: &mut) {
    while self.ngray > 0usize {
      self.ngray = self.ngray - 1usize;
      self.scanObject(*(self.gray + self.ngray));
    }
  }

  // Has a mark-variant collection found the object at 'header'?
  fn reachedMark(header: usize) bool {
    let f = (header as &ActorGc).flags;
    if f & flagLarge != 0u32 {
      return f & (flagMarked | flagOld) != 0u32;
    }
    if f & flagForwarded != 0u32 {
      return true;
    }
    markedAt(header)
  }

  fn movedMark(header: usize) usize {
    let f = (header as &ActorGc).flags;
    if f & flagLarge == 0u32 && f & flagForwarded != 0u32 {
      return (header as &ActorGc).link;
    }
    header
  }

  // As finalizeDead: the objects with a final that nothing reached are kept
  // one more cycle, here by marking them in place with all they reach, and
  // their death is run once, newest first. They are not moved
  fn finalizeDeadMark(self: &mut) {
    if self.nfinals == 0usize {
      return;
    }
    let dead = libc.malloc(self.nfinals * mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
    if dead == null {
      panic("ActorGc: out of memory for a collection");
    }
    var ndead = 0usize;
    // a minor collection judges only the young finalizable objects (those after finalsOld); an old one is found by a full collection
    var start = 0usize;
    if stickyOn && self.isMinor {
      start = self.finalsOld;
    }
    var kept = start;
    var i = start;
    while i < self.nfinals {
      let header = *(self.finals + i);
      if ActorRegion.reachedMark(header) {
        *(self.finals + kept) = ActorRegion.movedMark(header);
        kept = kept + 1usize;
      } else {
        *(dead + ndead) = header;
        ndead = ndead + 1usize;
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    self.nfinals = kept;
    i = 0usize;
    while i < ndead {
      let header = *(dead + i);
      let h = header as &mut ActorGc;
      if h.flags & flagLarge != 0u32 {
        if h.flags & flagMarked == 0u32 {
          h.flags = h.flags | flagMarked;
          self.liveNow = self.liveNow + usize.from(h.size);
          self.pushGray(header);
        }
      } else if self.markIn(header, header & (0usize - blockBytes), usize.from(h.size)) {
        self.nmarked = self.nmarked + 1usize;
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    self.drainMark();
    i = ndead;
    while i > 0usize {
      i = i - 1usize;
      let header = *(dead + i);
      let rec = (header as &ActorGc).ty as &TypeRecord;
      let fin = rec.finalize;
      fin((header + ActorRegion.valueOffset(rec)) as *u8);
      self.nfinalized = self.nfinalized + 1usize;
    }
    libc.free(dead as *u8);
  }

  // Before marking (GC_RECLAIM 2): if the free room inside partly used blocks
  // passed evacFrag per cent at the last sweep, the blocks that sweep left
  // recyclable, that nothing has allocated in since, and that are under
  // evacSparse per cent live, are evacuated in this collection
  fn chooseCandidates(self: &mut) {
    self.evacOn = false;
    if self.fragPct < evacFrag {
      return;
    }
    let room = (linesPerBlock - firstLine) * lineBytes;
    var n = 0usize;
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < self.nbk {
      let info = *(self.bk + i) as &mut BlockInfo;
      if info.flags & flagRecyclable != 0u32 && usize.from(info.live) * 100usize < room * evacSparse {
        info.flags = info.flags | flagCandidate;
        n = n + 1usize;
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    self.nevacBlocks = self.nevacBlocks + n;
    self.evacOn = n > 0usize;
    if n > 0usize {
      self.nevacColl = self.nevacColl + 1usize;
    }
  }

  // The collection of the mark variants: nothing moves unless a block is
  // being evacuated
  fn collectMark(self: &mut, st: *u8, rec: *TypeRecord) {
    self.epoch = self.epoch + 1u32;
    if self.epoch == 0u32 {
      self.epoch = 1u32;
    }
    curActorRegion = self as *ActorRegion;
    self.evacCursor = 0usize;
    self.evacLimit = 0usize;
    self.evacOn = false;
    if reclaimMode == 2usize {
      self.chooseCandidates();
    }
    let minor = stickyOn && self.isMinor;
    if !minor {
      // Clear the side tables and the blocks' live bytes
      var i = 0usize;
      while i < self.nbk {
        let b = *(self.bk + i);
        libc.memset((b + lineOff) as *u8, 0, bitmapOff + 256usize - lineOff);
        (b as &mut BlockInfo).live = 0u32;
        i = i + 1usize;
      }
      self.liveNow = 0usize;
      self.nobjects = 0usize;
      if stickyOn {
        // every large object is young again, until this collection keeps it
        var at = self.large;
        while at != 0usize {
          let h = at as &mut ActorGc;
          h.flags = h.flags & ~flagOld;
          at = h.link;
        }
      }
    } else {
      // a minor collection keeps the marks and the live counts of the last: only what it finds is added
      self.nobjects = self.nobjBase;
    }
    if self.nrem > self.remPeak {
      self.remPeak = self.nrem;
    }
    let r = rec as &TypeRecord;
    if r.flags & 2u32 != 0u32 {
      let trace = r.trace;
      trace(st, self.epoch);
    }
    if stickyOn {
      // The remembered set: old objects written to since the last collection. A minor collection scans each (what it holds that is
      // young is then marked); a full one traces everything anyway. Either way the set is emptied and the log flags cleared
      var k = 0usize;
      while k < self.nrem {
        let hd = *(self.rem + k);
        (hd as &mut ActorGc).flags = (hd as &ActorGc).flags & ~flagLogged;
        if minor {
          self.scanObject(hd);
        }
        k = k + 1usize;
      }
      self.nrem = 0usize;
    }
    self.drainMark();
    self.finalizeDeadMark();
    if minor {
      self.sweepLargeYoung();
      self.sweepTouched();
    } else {
      self.sweepLarge();
      self.sweepBlocks();
      self.ntch = 0usize;
    }
    self.finalsOld = self.nfinals;
    self.stickyReady = true;
    self.ncollections = self.ncollections + 1usize;
    self.live = self.liveNow + self.blocks;
    self.liveHeap = self.liveNow;
    self.allocated = 0usize;
    if !minor {
      self.nobjects = self.nobjects + self.nlarge;
    }
    self.nobjBase = self.nobjects;
    curActorRegion = null;
  }

  // (GC_STICKY, minor) The young large objects are at the head of the list, as new ones are put there: free those not reached, keep and
  // age the others, and stop at the first old one
  fn sweepLargeYoung(self: &mut) {
    var prev = 0usize;
    var at = self.large;
    var kept = 0usize;
    while at != 0usize {
      let h = at as &mut ActorGc;
      if h.flags & flagOld != 0u32 {
        break;
      }
      let next = h.link;
      if h.flags & flagMarked == 0u32 {
        if prev == 0usize {
          self.large = next;
        } else {
          (prev as &mut ActorGc).link = next;
        }
        RawBlocks.release(at as *u8);
        self.nfreed = self.nfreed + 1usize;
        self.nlarge = self.nlarge - 1usize;
      } else {
        h.flags = (h.flags & ~flagMarked) | flagOld;
        kept = kept + 1usize;
        prev = at;
      }
      at = next;
    }
    self.nobjects = self.nobjects + kept;
  }

  // (GC_STICKY, minor) Only the blocks the allocator took since the last collection are swept: empty ones join the free list, the
  // rest the recycle list if they have a free line. Blocks it did not take are as the last sweep left them (and are not given back
  // here: a full collection does that)
  fn sweepTouched(self: &mut) {
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < self.ntch {
      let b = *(self.tch + i);
      let info = b as &mut BlockInfo;
      info.flags = 0u32;
      info.next = 0usize;
      if info.live == 0u32 {
        info.next = self.freeList;
        self.freeList = b;
        self.nfree = self.nfree + 1usize;
      } else {
        let marks = (b + lineOff) as *u8;
        var free = 0usize;
        var l = firstLine;
        while l < linesPerBlock {
          if *(marks + l) == 0u8 {
            free = free + 1usize;
          }
          l = l + 1usize;
        }
        if free > 0usize {
          info.flags = flagRecyclable;
          info.next = self.recycle;
          self.recycle = b;
        }
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    self.ntch = 0usize;
    self.nspare = self.nfree * blockBytes;
    self.curBlock = 0usize;
    self.curLine = 0usize;
    self.cursor = 0usize;
    self.limit = 0usize;
  }

  // (GC_STICKY) The workload's write barrier: 'o' has had a reference stored into one of its fields. If 'o' is old (kept by a collection)
  // and not yet logged, it joins the remembered set, which the next minor collection scans. A young object needs nothing. Call it after
  // each such store, through the actor's region: 'self.allocRegion().wrote(o)'
  pub fn wrote[T](self: &mut, o: ActorGc[mut, T]) {
    if !stickyOn {
      return;
    }
    self.nbarrier = self.nbarrier + 1usize;
    let header = (o as *u8 as usize) - ActorRegion.roundUp(mem.sizeof[ActorGc](), mem.alignof[T]());
    let h = header as &mut ActorGc;
    if h.flags & flagLogged != 0u32 {
      return;
    }
    var old = false;
    if h.flags & flagLarge != 0u32 {
      old = h.flags & flagOld != 0u32;
    } else {
      old = markedAt(header);
    }
    if !old {
      return;
    }
    h.flags = h.flags | flagLogged;
    if self.nrem == self.caprem {
      var cap = 256usize;
      if self.caprem != 0usize {
        cap = self.caprem * 2usize;
      }
      let grown = libc.realloc(self.rem as *u8, cap * mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
      if grown == null {
        panic("ActorGc: out of memory for the remembered set");
      }
      self.rem = grown;
      self.caprem = cap;
    }
    *(self.rem + self.nrem) = header;
    self.nrem = self.nrem + 1usize;
    self.nlogged = self.nlogged + 1usize;
    if self.nrem > self.remPeak {
      self.remPeak = self.nrem;
    }
  }

  // After marking: each block is empty (kept on the free list up to twice the
  // live bytes, else given back), full, or recyclable (listed, in block
  // order). The fragmentation measure for the next collection comes from here
  fn sweepBlocks(self: &mut) {
    let maxFree = (self.liveNow * 2usize) / blockBytes + 2usize;
    let room = (linesPerBlock - firstLine) * lineBytes;
    self.recycle = 0usize;
    self.freeList = 0usize;
    self.nfree = 0usize;
    var tail = 0usize;
    var out = 0usize;
    var usedRoom = 0usize;
    var freeRoom = 0usize;
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < self.nbk {
      let b = *(self.bk + i);
      let info = b as &mut BlockInfo;
      info.flags = 0u32;
      info.next = 0usize;
      if info.live == 0u32 {
        if self.nfree < maxFree {
          info.next = self.freeList;
          self.freeList = b;
          self.nfree = self.nfree + 1usize;
          *(self.bk + out) = b;
          out = out + 1usize;
        } else {
          let raw = info.raw;
          RawBlocks.release(raw as *u8);
        }
      } else {
        let marks = (b + lineOff) as *u8;
        var free = 0usize;
        var l = firstLine;
        while l < linesPerBlock {
          if *(marks + l) == 0u8 {
            free = free + 1usize;
          }
          l = l + 1usize;
        }
        usedRoom = usedRoom + room;
        if free > 0usize {
          freeRoom = freeRoom + free * lineBytes;
          info.flags = flagRecyclable;
          if tail == 0usize {
            self.recycle = b;
          } else {
            (tail as &mut BlockInfo).next = b;
          }
          tail = b;
        }
        *(self.bk + out) = b;
        out = out + 1usize;
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    self.nbk = out;
    self.nchunks = out;
    self.bytes = out * blockBytes;
    self.nspare = self.nfree * blockBytes;
    self.fragPct = 0usize;
    if usedRoom > 0usize {
      self.fragPct = (freeRoom * 100usize) / usedRoom;
    }
    self.curBlock = 0usize;
    self.curLine = 0usize;
    self.cursor = 0usize;
    self.limit = 0usize;
    self.evacCursor = 0usize;
    self.evacLimit = 0usize;
  }

  // ---- The check (GC_CHECK) ----

  // After a collection: walk everything reachable from the roots, and panic
  // at the first reference that is not to a live object in a known block
  fn verify(self: &mut, st: *u8, rec: *TypeRecord) {
    // The known places. Copying: every object in the chunks, found by walking
    // them. Mark variants: the blocks. Both: the large objects
    var nknown = self.nbk;
    if reclaimMode == 0usize {
      nknown = self.nobjects;
    }
    self.vkcap = setCap(nknown);
    self.vknown = setMake(self.vkcap);
    self.vlcap = setCap(self.nlarge);
    self.vlarge = setMake(self.vlcap);
    self.vcap = setCap(self.nobjects + self.nlarge);
    self.vset = setMake(self.vcap);
    if reclaimMode == 0usize {
      var c = self.first;
      while c != null {
        var off = mem.sizeof[Chunk]();
        let used = (c as &Chunk).used;
        while off < used {
          let at = (c as usize) + off;
          let size = usize.from((at as &ActorGc).size);
          if size == 0usize || size % 16usize != 0usize {
            panic("ActorGc check: a chunk holds an object with a bad size");
          }
          setAdd(self.vknown, self.vkcap, at);
          off = off + size;
        }
        c = (c as &Chunk).next;
      }
    } else {
      var i = 0usize;
      while i < self.nbk {
        setAdd(self.vknown, self.vkcap, *(self.bk + i));
        i = i + 1usize;
      }
    }
    var nl = 0usize;
    var at = self.large;
    while at != 0usize {
      setAdd(self.vlarge, self.vlcap, at);
      nl = nl + 1usize;
      at = (at as &ActorGc).link;
    }
    if nl != self.nlarge {
      panic("ActorGc check: the large list and the large count disagree");
    }
    // The final list: each entry a live object
    var i = 0usize;
    while i < self.nfinals {
      self.checkObject(*(self.finals + i));
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    // The walk from the roots
    self.epoch = self.epoch + 1u32;
    if self.epoch == 0u32 {
      self.epoch = 1u32;
    }
    self.verifying = true;
    curActorRegion = self as *ActorRegion;
    let r = rec as &TypeRecord;
    if r.flags & 2u32 != 0u32 {
      let trace = r.trace;
      trace(st, self.epoch);
    }
    while self.ngray > 0usize {
      self.ngray = self.ngray - 1usize;
      self.scanObject(*(self.gray + self.ngray));
    }
    self.verifying = false;
    curActorRegion = null;
    libc.free(self.vknown as *u8);
    libc.free(self.vlarge as *u8);
    libc.free(self.vset as *u8);
    self.vknown = null;
    self.vlarge = null;
    self.vset = null;
  }

  // Is 'header' a live object in a known place, with a header that makes sense?
  fn checkObject(self: &mut, header: usize) {
    var ok = false;
    var large = false;
    if setHas(self.vlarge, self.vlcap, header) {
      ok = true;
      large = true;
    } else if reclaimMode == 0usize {
      ok = setHas(self.vknown, self.vkcap, header);
    } else {
      let blk = header & (0usize - blockBytes);
      if setHas(self.vknown, self.vkcap, blk) {
        if header < blk + blockMeta {
          panic("ActorGc check: a reference into a block's record");
        }
        if !markedAt(header) {
          panic("ActorGc check: a reference to an object that is not marked live");
        }
        ok = true;
      }
    }
    if !ok {
      panic("ActorGc check: a reference to a place that is not a live object in a known block");
    }
    let h = header as &ActorGc;
    if h.flags & flagForwarded != 0u32 {
      panic("ActorGc check: a reference to a forwarded object");
    }
    if h.ty == null {
      panic("ActorGc check: an object with no type record");
    }
    if usize.from(h.size) % 16usize != 0usize {
      panic("ActorGc check: an object with a size that is not a multiple of 16");
    }
    let t = h.ty as &TypeRecord;
    if usize.from(h.size) != ActorRegion.roundUp(ActorRegion.valueOffset(t) + t.size, 16usize) {
      panic("ActorGc check: an object whose size does not fit its type");
    }
    if large != (h.flags & flagLarge != 0u32) {
      panic("ActorGc check: the large flag and the large list disagree");
    }
  }

  // The walk found a reference to the object at 'header': check it once, and
  // have it scanned
  fn verifyRef(self: &mut, header: usize) {
    if !setAdd(self.vset, self.vcap, header) {
      return;
    }
    self.checkObject(header);
    self.pushGray(header);
  }

  // The actor has died, its state finalized: every object on the final list
  // finalized, newest first, and the region freed
  fn die(self: &mut) {
    var i = self.nfinals;
    while i > 0usize {
      i = i - 1usize;
      let header = *(self.finals + i);
      let rec = (header as &ActorGc).ty as &TypeRecord;
      let fin = rec.finalize;
      fin((header + ActorRegion.valueOffset(rec)) as *u8);
      self.nfinalized = self.nfinalized + 1usize;
    }
    var c = self.first;
    while c != null {
      let next = (c as &Chunk).next;
      RawBlocks.release(c as *u8);
      c = next;
    }
    c = self.spare;
    while c != null {
      let next = (c as &Chunk).next;
      RawBlocks.release(c as *u8);
      c = next;
    }
    self.spare = null;
    self.nspare = 0usize;
    var at = self.large;
    while at != 0usize {
      let next = (at as &ActorGc).link;
      RawBlocks.release(at as *u8);
      at = next;
    }
    var k = 0usize;
    while k < self.nbk {
      let raw = (*(self.bk + k) as &BlockInfo).raw;
      RawBlocks.release(raw as *u8);
      k = k + 1usize;
    }
    self.nbk = 0usize;
    libc.free(self.bk as *u8);
    libc.free(self.finals as *u8);
    libc.free(self.gray as *u8);
    libc.free(self.rem as *u8);
    libc.free(self.tch as *u8);
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The region as a MemRegion, and what the message loop calls
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The region functions a collection made in the region calls, each handed the
// MemRegion it holds, which is the 'vt' field of the actor's ActorRegion: one
// pointer past its start
fn blockRegion(r: *MemRegion) &mut ActorRegion {
  ((r as usize - mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *ActorRegion) as &mut ActorRegion
}

fn blockAlloc(r: *MemRegion, bytes: usize, align: usize) *u8 {
  blockRegion(r).blockNew(bytes, align)
}

fn blockResize(r: *MemRegion, block: *u8, oldBytes: usize, newBytes: usize, align: usize) *u8 {
  blockRegion(r).blockGrow(block, oldBytes, newBytes, align)
}

fn blockRelease(r: *MemRegion, block: *u8, bytes: usize) {
  blockRegion(r).blockGive(block, bytes);
}

// The actor's region gives exactly what is asked
fn blockSize(r: *MemRegion, bytes: usize, align: usize) usize { bytes }

// A message has finished: collect, if the bytes allocated since the last
// collection exceed what survived it, and are not under the floor (or if stress
// is on). Only an actor that has asked for its region comes here
fn collectIfDue(g: &mut ActorRegion, st: *u8, rec: *TypeRecord) {
  var want = stressOn.load();
  if stickyOn && !want {
    want = g.allocated > nurseryBytes;
  } else if !want {
    var threshold = floorBytes.load();
    var f = g.factor100;
    if f == 0usize {
      f = factorInit;
    }
    // The heap may grow to f/100 times what survived: allocate (f - 100)/100 times that
    let grow = (g.live * (f - 100usize)) / 100usize;
    if grow > threshold {
      threshold = grow;
    }
    want = g.allocated > threshold;
  }
  if want {
    g.collect(st, rec);
  }
}

// The actor's mailbox has emptied (GC_EMPTY): collect if it has allocated past
// a small floor since the last collection. No message waits for it
fn collectIfEmpty(g: &mut ActorRegion, st: *u8, rec: *TypeRecord) {
  var th = emptyFloor;
  if emptyPct > 0usize {
    var f = g.factor100;
    if f == 0usize {
      f = factorInit;
    }
    let part = (g.live * (f - 100usize) / 100usize) * emptyPct / 100usize;
    if part > th {
      th = part;
    }
  }
  if g.allocated > th {
    g.collect(st, rec);
  }
}

// The actor's region, for the state's generated 'allocRegion': made now, the
// first time it is asked for, and kept by the actor's Header until its death
pub fn regionOf[S, M: Sendable](st: &mut S) &'static mut ActorRegion {
  let h = headerOf[S, M](st);
  if (*h).gc as usize == 0usize {
    (*h).gc = ActorRegion.create();
  }
  (*h).gc as &mut ActorRegion
}
